    "title": "Hottest September on record across the Ozarks - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Jamie Warriner ,\n2 hours ago\nIt's official: September 2026 was the hottest recorded across the Ozarks. Springfield beat the previous record holder, 2019, by a whopping 2.3°! That's shattering the previous record. Remember, this is an average of all of the temperatures from the beginning to the end, lows and highs. All of the other reporting sites also recorded record-warm Septembers.\nIt was amazing how hot it was during the day, but also at night. Springfield recorded the most overnight lows of 70° or greater in September. On the high end, it was the second-most days of 90° or greater. Five 100° days were recorded in Springfield, the most ever in September.\nThe main reason for the heat was a persistent heat dome over the Southern Plains. This kept heat in the area while deterring cold fronts from moving in. The dryness at the end of summer also played a role, making it much easier to max out afternoon highs.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed.\nFor the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KOLR - OzarksFirst.com.",
